<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03RS">Example <span data-tag="03RS">4.9.3.15</span>.</a> Let $(T, \leq )$ be a nonempty linearly ordered set with no largest element. Then the cofinality $\kappa = \mathrm{cf}(T)$ is a regular cardinal. To see this, choose a well-ordered set $(S, \leq )$ of order type $\kappa $ and a cofinal function $f: S \rightarrow T$. Proposition <a data-tag="03RA" href="/tag/03RA">4.9.2.15</a> guarantees that $\kappa $ is a cardinal, and Example <a data-tag="03QR" href="/tag/03QR">4.9.1.31</a> shows that $\kappa $ is infinite. If it is not regular, then there exists a cofinal map $g: R \rightarrow S$, where $(R, \leq )$ is a well-ordered set of order type $\alpha &lt; \kappa $. This contradicts the definition of $\kappa = \mathrm{cf}(T)$, since the composite map $(f \circ g): R \rightarrow T$ is cofinal. </p>
